V2 Architecture Document — Part 3: Business Rules and System Logic

This is where the schema becomes a real product.

Part 2 defined the tables.
Part 3 defines how the system behaves.

Without this, you just have a database.
With this, you have an operating system.

1. Core rule engine

Your v2 should run on 5 business engines:

Access engine
Membership engine
Attendance engine
Workout engine
Platform control engine

Later you add:
6. Retention / intelligence engine

2. Access engine

This controls who can do what.

2.1 Roles
Platform roles
super_admin
platform_support
platform_ops

These roles live in:

platform_admins

They are platform-only.
They are not gym roles.

Gym roles
gym_owner
gym_admin
front_desk
trainer

These live in:

gym_user_roles

They are gym-scoped.

Member state

A member is not a staff role.
A member is a business entity in:

members

A member may optionally be linked to a login profile.

2.2 Permission model
Super admin can:
create gyms
suspend gyms
assign plans
override features
view all gyms
impersonate gym context
view global analytics
view subscription state
Platform support can:
inspect gyms
impersonate
view logs
assist debugging

But may not:

change pricing
delete platform records without authorization
Gym owner can:
manage staff
manage members
create membership types
manage workouts
see gym analytics
update gym branding/settings
Gym admin can:
manage members
manage workouts
manage attendance
view analytics
manage selected staff actions

But may be restricted from:

changing owner-only billing/config
Front desk can:
search members
scan QR
validate membership
check in/out members
see limited member status

But cannot:

manage plans
edit workouts
view full analytics
view platform data
Trainer can:
view assigned members
assign workout plans
create/update training programs
log workout completion
view limited member progress

But cannot:

change billing
manage platform settings
manage all staff unless allowed
Member can:
see own membership status
see own workouts
see own attendance history
log own workout sessions
receive notifications

But can never:

see other members
access gym admin data
access platform data
2.3 Hard access rules

These must be enforced server-side.

Rule A

A request must always resolve:

who is the actor
which gym they are acting inside
which role they hold in that gym
Rule B

No gym-scoped query should trust the frontend’s claimed gym_id blindly.

The backend must verify:

actor has access to this gym
actor role permits this action
Rule C

Platform admins operate outside tenant RLS assumptions, but support actions must be logged.

Rule D

Members only access their own data, even if linked to a profile.

3. Membership engine

This is one of the biggest upgrades from v1.

Your current system does not have a real member membership lifecycle.
V2 must.

3.1 Membership lifecycle states

Each member can exist in these business states:

lead
trial
active
inactive
expired
blocked

This is member state in members.status.

Separate from that, each commercial membership in member_memberships.status can be:

active
expired
paused
cancelled

These are related but not identical.

3.2 Key distinction

A member can exist without an active membership.

Examples:

lead captured but never paid
expired member still in system
blocked member
past customer
trial user not converted

This matters for analytics and reactivation later.

3.3 Membership validation logic

Whenever attendance or access is checked, the backend must determine:

does member exist in this gym
is member blocked
is there an active membership row
is today between start_date and end_date
is payment status acceptable
is attendance limit exceeded, if such rules exist
Valid membership

Member can check in.

Expired membership

Member cannot check in automatically.

Paused membership

Member cannot check in.

Unpaid membership

Business rule configurable:

deny
warn but allow
allow for grace period

This should become gym-configurable later.

3.4 Renewal logic

A membership approaching expiry should surface:

expiring in 7 days
expiring in 3 days
expired today
expired and inactive

This powers:

front desk alerts
admin dashboard notices
future reminders
4. Attendance engine

This is not just a log table.
This is a validation system.

4.1 Attendance flow

Core flow:

identify member
identify gym and branch
validate membership
validate member status
create attendance outcome
optionally create checkout later
emit analytics and audit events
4.2 Member identification methods

Support these methods:

qr
manual
staff
kiosk

Later you can add:

NFC
biometric
phone number lookup
membership code
4.3 Check-in outcomes

A check-in attempt can end in:

entered
denied
completed later after checkout

Denial reasons may include:

membership expired
member blocked
invalid QR
wrong gym
duplicate same-session scan
branch restriction
unpaid membership

These should be stored, not ignored.

4.4 Duplicate logic

You need a clear policy.

Recommended rule:

allow only one active open check-in per member per branch at a time
optionally allow multiple visits per day if they checked out
configurable later if gym wants strict one-visit-per-day

Do not lock yourself into the current simplistic unique-per-day model.

4.5 Checkout logic

Checkout should be optional but supported.

Why it matters:

duration analytics
accurate active-in-gym view
class/session monitoring
peak hour analysis

If a gym does not use checkout, system still works.
If they do, analytics becomes stronger.

4.6 Front desk behavior

Front desk mode should return instantly:

Success response
member name
photo
membership valid
plan name
expiry date
visits this month
successful check-in time
Denied response
member name if identifiable
exact denial reason
next action prompt

Example:

“Membership expired 2 days ago”
“Blocked member — contact admin”
“Wrong branch access”
4.7 Attendance business rules
Rule A

Attendance belongs to member_id, not profile_id.

Rule B

Every attendance row belongs to exactly one gym.

Rule C

Front desk attribution should be captured when staff performs check-in.

Rule D

All attendance actions that matter should be auditable.

